WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.667
A filter response combines a local patch with a kernel. Multiply aligned entries and add their

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:13.135
contributions to obtain one output. Sliding the kernel repeats this calculation at other positions, which makes

00:00:13.135 --> 00:00:15.560
the weights shared across the image.

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:21.331
The order of entries matters. Fix which end of the patch aligns with which end of

00:00:21.331 --> 00:00:22.647
the kernel before multiplying.

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:31.267
Cross-correlation multiplies aligned entries directly. This is the operation many deep-learning convolution layers implement.

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:37.729
Would a symmetric kernel reveal the difference between the two conventions? Pause here and make a

00:00:37.729 --> 00:00:38.847
prediction before continuing.

00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:48.447
In this finite local illustration, convolution reverses kernel order before alignment. In two dimensions it reverses

00:00:48.447 --> 00:00:54.807
both spatial axes. For our starting inputs, Cross-correlation: -2. Flipped-kernel response: 2.

00:00:55.333 --> 00:01:00.983
The responses differ because this kernel is asymmetric. Symmetric kernels hide the distinction.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:07.260
Track the alignment and flip convention before interpreting a filter response as an edge or feature.

